["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"Don&#x27;t Teach Your Kid Empathy; First Let Them See You Listening Getting kids to disagree well is not a matter of teaching empathy or perspective-taking — it is a matter of proving with words that you are listening; the HEAR framework and the curiosity sandwich can be used immediately. Raising Good Humans Raising Good Humans How to Disagree With Your Kids Without Fighting Parent-child talk Disagreement Listening Harvard research Conversation frameworks Parenting HEAR framework curiosity sandwich false polarization hedging\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-raisinggh-别只让孩子换位思考-要让他看见你在听/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/41f3114355314dc3-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"R\"><span class=\"dur\">55:22</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Raising Good Humans</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>Don&#x27;t Teach Your Kid Empathy; First Let Them See You Listening</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Getting kids to disagree well is not a matter of teaching empathy or perspective-taking — it is a matter of proving with words that you are listening; the HEAR framework and the curiosity sandwich can be used immediately.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Parent-child talk</span><span class=\"tag\">Disagreement</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Screening embryos isn&#x27;t doing everything possible — it&#x27;s moral blackmail Genetic screening can lower disease risk, but the statistics are routinely oversold, and the practice may deepen social inequality. Parents should aim for &quot;good enough&quot; rather than optimal, and stay wary of the moral blackmail commercial firms trade on. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area What does a parent owe their child? Genetic screening Reproductive ethics IVF Polygenic testing Value pluralism PGT-A polygenic testing pleiotropy satisficing\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-grayarea-胚胎筛选-父母该为孩子做到哪一步/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/77b28234367aefc8-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">27:47</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>Screening embryos isn&#x27;t doing everything possible — it&#x27;s moral blackmail</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Genetic screening can lower disease risk, but the statistics are routinely oversold, and the practice may deepen social inequality. Parents should aim for &quot;good enough&quot; rather than optimal, and stay wary of the moral blackmail commercial firms trade on.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Genetic screening</span><span class=\"tag\">Reproductive ethics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"An Actress&#x27;s Contract Locked In Her Body: Gain Weight and You Breach It, Lose Weight and It Kills You In the 1920s the actress Molly was shelved by her studio for gaining weight and ended up on an operating table; in the 1990s a bride named Mary died from the diet drug fen-phen. Once a body becomes a commercial asset, the punishment and the shortcut lead to the same place. Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford Cautionary Tales The Weight of Fame: A Hollywood Tragedy Hollywood Body standards Diet drugs Business ethics Contract terms Medical risk lipectomy fen-phen primary pulmonary hypertension (PPH)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-cautionary-制片厂的合同里写着-女演员的身体不得改变/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b996da73a08744ac-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">41:39</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>An Actress&#x27;s Contract Locked In Her Body: Gain Weight and You Breach It, Lose Weight and It Kills You</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In the 1920s the actress Molly was shelved by her studio for gaining weight and ended up on an operating table; in the 1990s a bride named Mary died from the diet drug fen-phen. Once a body becomes a commercial asset, the punishment and the shortcut lead to the same place.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Hollywood</span><span class=\"tag\">Body standards</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AMD Buys Talus: Burning AI Models Into Silicon, 17,000 Tokens a Second AMD has acquired Talus, a startup that builds custom chips for one specific AI model each and reaches 17,000 tokens per second. It marks a turn in AI inference toward non-programmable, model-specific silicon. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato Why did AMD just buy this REALLY WEIRD chip company? AI chips AMD Talus Model customization Inference acceleration SRAM reticle size agentic AI\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-techtechpotato-amd收购talus-押注模型定制芯片的未来/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/0d473494a95916b2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">16:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>AMD Buys Talus: Burning AI Models Into Silicon, 17,000 Tokens a Second</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AMD has acquired Talus, a startup that builds custom chips for one specific AI model each and reaches 17,000 tokens per second. It marks a turn in AI inference toward non-programmable, model-specific silicon.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI chips</span><span class=\"tag\">AMD</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI Didn&#x27;t Kill Jobs — Agent Teamwork Is the Real Bottleneck Yangqing Jia (贾扬清) traces fifteen years of AI from Caffe through two startups: model capability is already good enough, and the bottleneck is getting a group of agents to collaborate the way a human team does. He also argues flatly that AI has not caused mass layoffs — it is just the scapegoat. What&#x27;s Next｜科技早知道 科技早知道 贾扬清：我所经历的「人工智能已死」到「AI 颠覆世界」的数年巨变丨串台「声东击西」S10E24 AI Startups Open source Agents Silicon Valley LLMs Caffe AlexNet Agent FAIR\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-whatsnext-单个智能体再强也成不了团队-ai需要生产关系/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/98eaf4baa4b6920b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">2:10:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">What&#x27;s Next｜科技早知道</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>AI Didn&#x27;t Kill Jobs — Agent Teamwork Is the Real Bottleneck</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Yangqing Jia (<span lang=\"zh\">贾扬清</span>) traces fifteen years of AI from Caffe through two startups: model capability is already good enough, and the bottleneck is getting a group of agents to collaborate the way a human team does. He also argues flatly that AI has not caused mass layoffs — it is just the scapegoat.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Startups</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Yo-yo dieting doesn&#x27;t wreck your metabolism; habits, not weight, decide lifespan Weight cycling — yo-yo dieting — does not permanently break your metabolism, and in ordinary people the weight that comes back rarely costs you muscle. What actually shapes health and lifespan is the healthy habits themselves, not the swings in weight. Science Vs Science Vs Weight Loss: Why Won&#x27;t It Stick? Weight cycling Weight loss Metabolism Inflammation Healthy habits Randomized trials weight cycling visceral fat macrophage\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-sciencevs-体重循环不毁代谢-健康行为比体重重要/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a3282246c3202bd6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">39:39</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>Yo-yo dieting doesn&#x27;t wreck your metabolism; habits, not weight, decide lifespan</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Weight cycling — yo-yo dieting — does not permanently break your metabolism, and in ordinary people the weight that comes back rarely costs you muscle. What actually shapes health and lifespan is the healthy habits themselves, not the swings in weight.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Weight cycling</span><span class=\"tag\">Weight loss</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"The US joins Japan to intervene on the yen: selling euros, not dollars, and betting the BOJ hikes In a rare move, the US Treasury has intervened on the yen alongside Japan, selling euros rather than dollars and putting the FIMA repo facility to use. Brad Setser argues the intervention will work as long as the Bank of Japan raises rates, and that Japan&#x27;s fiscal position is far better than America&#x27;s. Odd Lots Odd Lots Brad Setser on the US&#x27;s Unusual Japanese Yen Intervention Yen FX intervention Bank of Japan Treasuries Fiscal policy FIMA Repo Facility ESF (Exchange Stabilization Fund) Primary Balance Carry Trade Big Mac Index\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-oddlots-美国为何联手日本干预日元-新工具与旧逻辑/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">42:00</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>The US joins Japan to intervene on the yen: selling euros, not dollars, and betting the BOJ hikes</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a rare move, the US Treasury has intervened on the yen alongside Japan, selling euros rather than dollars and putting the FIMA repo facility to use. Brad Setser argues the intervention will work as long as the Bank of Japan raises rates, and that Japan&#x27;s fiscal position is far better than America&#x27;s.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Yen</span><span class=\"tag\">FX intervention</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"222-nanometre lamps kill pathogens and still sell a few hundred a year; the bottleneck isn&#x27;t the technology Far UVC uses 222-nanometre light to inactivate pathogens in the air while leaving the outer layer of human tissue unharmed; a South African TB trial is already seeing 90% suppression, yet the whole world buys only a few hundred units a year. The real bottleneck is how far the knowledge has spread. Complex Systems Complex Systems The economics of putting germicidal light in every room, with Misha Gurevich and Vivian Belenky of Aerolamp far UVC airborne disease public health startups building codes cost curves far UVC krypton chloride excimer lamp ASHRAE 241 R0 hygiene hypothesis\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-complexsys-222纳米杀菌灯便宜有效-缺的是认知而非技术/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7fc764da6acb74e8-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>222-nanometre lamps kill pathogens and still sell a few hundred a year; the bottleneck isn&#x27;t the technology</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Far UVC uses 222-nanometre light to inactivate pathogens in the air while leaving the outer layer of human tissue unharmed; a South African TB trial is already seeing 90% suppression, yet the whole world buys only a few hundred units a year. The real bottleneck is how far the knowledge has spread.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">far UVC</span><span class=\"tag\">airborne disease</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"The Soviet Union forged its women into supermen and spoiled its men into uselessness 27 million Soviet citizens died in the Second World War, and men became a scarce resource; the state used an abortion ban, rewards for children fathered outside marriage, and the decriminalization of domestic violence to forge its women into supermen and spoil its men into uselessness — an order that still underwrites Putin&#x27;s patriarchal mobilization. Conversations with Tyler Conversations with Tyler Julia Ioffe on Russian Women, Missing Men, and the Roots of Autocracy Russian society Gender politics Population policy Feminist history Putin politics woman question single sex family gender essentialism pronatalist policy steam valve\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-05-tyler-苏联把女人炼成超人-把男人宠成废物/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/3096d9c0d3a4a23d-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">55:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Conversations with Tyler</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0805</span></div>\n<h2>The Soviet Union forged its women into supermen and spoiled its men into uselessness</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">27 million Soviet citizens died in the Second World War, and men became a scarce resource; the state used an abortion ban, rewards for children fathered outside marriage, and the decriminalization of domestic violence to forge its women into supermen and spoil its men into uselessness — an order that still underwrites Putin&#x27;s patriarchal mobilization.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Russian society</span><span class=\"tag\">Gender politics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Big Tech&#x27;s off-balance-sheet debt isn&#x27;t fraud — nobody reads the footnotes The supposed $1.65 trillion of hidden debt is not Enron-style fraud but long-term leases on data centres that aren&#x27;t running yet, plus purchase commitments — legal, but buried in the footnotes. The real danger is that the AI giants are financing each other and telling their story through adjusted earnings. Patrick Boyle Patrick Boyle Big Tech&#x27;s Hidden Debt Problem AI capex Off-balance-sheet debt Accounting gloss Footnote disclosure Valuation bubble off-balance-sheet vehicle vendor financing big market delusion incomplete revelation hypothesis limits of arbitrage\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-04-patrickboyle-表外债务不是欺诈-真正的风险是没人读脚注/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2eb71149af422d0e-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">32:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Patrick Boyle</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0804</span></div>\n<h2>Big Tech&#x27;s off-balance-sheet debt isn&#x27;t fraud — nobody reads the footnotes</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The supposed $1.65 trillion of hidden debt is not Enron-style fraud but long-term leases on data centres that aren&#x27;t running yet, plus purchase commitments — legal, but buried in the footnotes. The real danger is that the AI giants are financing each other and telling their story through adjusted earnings.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI capex</span><span class=\"tag\">Off-balance-sheet debt</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI revenue up 700%, but on grunt work AI still trails humans 6x The 2026 evidence on AI progress: revenue exploding, coding agents maturing, grunt-work tasks still lagging; timelines have shortened by about a year, but the long run is still open. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours What the hell happened with AGI timelines in 2026? – Rob Wiblin AGI AI agents Revenue growth Grunt work Inference scaling Timelines RLVR METR ECI GDPval Vending-Bench 2 AI Village\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-04-eightythousand-agi-时间线提前一年-但脏活仍是瓶颈/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a7038cb60f3d75b6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">49:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0804</span></div>\n<h2>AI revenue up 700%, but on grunt work AI still trails humans 6x</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The 2026 evidence on AI progress: revenue exploding, coding agents maturing, grunt-work tasks still lagging; timelines have shortened by about a year, but the long run is still open.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AGI</span><span class=\"tag\">AI agents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"Getting cut from the friend group is not a verdict — it is chemical bonding Being frozen out by a friend group, or needing to end a friendship yourself, are both normal chapters of adolescence; the most useful thing a parent can do is hand the kid perspective and specific lines to say, not charge in on their behalf. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 283: ENCORE: Best of Ask Lisa: Friendship Breakups &amp; Conflict Adolescence Friendship Bullying Social conflict Parent-child communication relational aggression queen bee blame the parent two-faced\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-04-asklisa-友谊破裂是成长章节-不是人生判决/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ace27132dfc1e3aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">23:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0804</span></div>\n<h2>Getting cut from the friend group is not a verdict — it is chemical bonding</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Being frozen out by a friend group, or needing to end a friendship yourself, are both normal chapters of adolescence; the most useful thing a parent can do is hand the kid perspective and specific lines to say, not charge in on their behalf.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Adolescence</span><span class=\"tag\">Friendship</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"A $250,000 Salary Wasn&#x27;t Enough, So He Extorted His Own Company Ubiquiti&#x27;s cloud team lead decided $250,000 a year was not enough, staged an intrusion into his own company and demanded 25 bitcoin — and was pinned down by a MAC address logged by his own Ubiquiti router. Six years in prison. Darknet Diaries Darknet Diaries 178: Ubiquiti Insider Attack Corporate Extortion GitHub VPN Cloud Security Forensics least privilege VPN kill switch MAC address\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-04-darknetdiaries-ubiquiti-内鬼自导自演勒索比特币-败在自家路由器/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/e2627fbdeaef9327-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">36:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Darknet Diaries</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0804</span></div>\n<h2>A $250,000 Salary Wasn&#x27;t Enough, So He Extorted His Own Company</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ubiquiti&#x27;s cloud team lead decided $250,000 a year was not enough, staged an intrusion into his own company and demanded 25 bitcoin — and was pinned down by a MAC address logged by his own Ubiquiti router. Six years in prison.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Insider Attack</span><span class=\"tag\">Corporate Extortion</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Quantizing more layers can be more faithful: the errors cancel out Once you have picked the right layers, quantizing more of them actually preserves more fidelity — the errors cancel each other out. By the same logic, a 10x speedup is not one breakthrough: it is quantization, the speculator and PD disaggregation each roughly doubling and stacking up. Latent Space Latent Space The Inference Engineering Masterclass — Philip Kiely &amp; Ali Taha, Baseten Inference optimization Quantization Speculative decoding GPU hardware Video generation Open models speculative decoding (spec dec) PD disaggregation (prefill/decode separation) NVFP4 mega kernel expert parallelism KV cache compaction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-03-latentspace-量化更多层反而更准-因为误差会互相抵消/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/23103bc773bd5837-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:41:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0803</span></div>\n<h2>Quantizing more layers can be more faithful: the errors cancel out</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Once you have picked the right layers, quantizing more of them actually preserves more fidelity — the errors cancel each other out. By the same logic, a 10x speedup is not one breakthrough: it is quantization, the speculator and PD disaggregation each roughly doubling and stacking up.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inference optimization</span><span class=\"tag\">Quantization</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Palestine Is Not a National Cause but a Self-Reproducing Symbol-Extraction Machine Stop treating Palestine as a single national cause — inside the Middle East&#x27;s power structure it is a machine that converts suffering into an asset for every party involved: no centre, no king that could be killed, and no way out except clarity at the personal level. EconTalk EconTalk The Political Economy of Palestine (with Hussein Aboubakr Mansour) Middle East politics Rent-seeking Ideology Qatar Palestine Victimhood narrative autopoietic symbolic convertibility machine UNRWA teshuvah\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-03-econtalk-巴勒斯坦不是民族事业-而是自再生的政治经济榨取机器/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/f8da698e33560635-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"E\"><span class=\"dur\">1:27:41</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">EconTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0803</span></div>\n<h2>Palestine Is Not a National Cause but a Self-Reproducing Symbol-Extraction Machine</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Stop treating Palestine as a single national cause — inside the Middle East&#x27;s power structure it is a machine that converts suffering into an asset for every party involved: no centre, no king that could be killed, and no way out except clarity at the personal level.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Middle East politics</span><span class=\"tag\">Rent-seeking</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Tungsten&#x27;s Price Spike Is a Hundred-Year War Prediction Market: China Cuts Supply and It Goes Up Tenfold Tungsten&#x27;s supply-and-demand cycle behaves like a war prediction market: prices spike before wars and collapse after them. China controls eighty percent of supply, and the West could restart old mines for only tens of millions of dollars — but with no futures market, the projects are hard to finance. Odd Lots Odd Lots The Tungsten Market Is Warning of an Upcoming War Tungsten Critical Minerals Geopolitics Supply Chains Mining Investment War Economy Ammonium Paratungstate (APT) Dry Metric Ton Unit (DMTU) Tungsten Carbide Ionic Absorption Clays\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-03-oddlots-钨价暴涨背后-一场百年战争预测市场/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">44:26</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0803</span></div>\n<h2>Tungsten&#x27;s Price Spike Is a Hundred-Year War Prediction Market: China Cuts Supply and It Goes Up Tenfold</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Tungsten&#x27;s supply-and-demand cycle behaves like a war prediction market: prices spike before wars and collapse after them. China controls eighty percent of supply, and the West could restart old mines for only tens of millions of dollars — but with no futures market, the projects are hard to finance.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Tungsten</span><span class=\"tag\">Critical Minerals</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\" data-hay=\"The biggest financial gift to a child isn&#x27;t a college fund — it&#x27;s a business at 10 A 10-year-old earned $759 of retirement money from pet-sitting — not early hothousing, but real responsibility building systems thinking, boundaries, persistence and financial independence. Your Parenting Mojo Your Parenting Mojo Re-release: Raising an Entrepreneur (Without Taking Over Their Business) Kid entrepreneurship Financial literacy Roth IRA Self-directed learning Neurodiversity Hands-off parenting Roth IRA unschooling sliding scale pricing guide on the side intake form\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-03-parentmojo-给孩子最大的财务礼物不是大学基金-是让她10岁创业/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/70c340acb32275d2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">1:00:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Your Parenting Mojo</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0803</span></div>\n<h2>The biggest financial gift to a child isn&#x27;t a college fund — it&#x27;s a business at 10</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">A 10-year-old earned $759 of retirement money from pet-sitting — not early hothousing, but real responsibility building systems thinking, boundaries, persistence and financial independence.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Kid entrepreneurship</span><span class=\"tag\">Financial literacy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Regulating America&#x27;s Own AI Is Pointless Unless China&#x27;s Open Models Are Curbed Too If the US restrains only its own frontier models without suppressing China&#x27;s open models, the safety logic does not hold; the real decider is the compute stock and the ability to deliver. ChinaTalk ChinaTalk Jordan talks AI with Sebastian Mallaby on his show &#x27;The Spillover&#x27; US-China AI race Open weights Compute AI safety Export controls DeepSeek open weights distillation alignment tokenomics frontier lab\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-01-chinatalk-美国要慢可以-但得把中国开源一起管住/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/94fa934f85ccd79f-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:20:03</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">ChinaTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0801</span></div>\n<h2>Regulating America&#x27;s Own AI Is Pointless Unless China&#x27;s Open Models Are Curbed Too</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">If the US restrains only its own frontier models without suppressing China&#x27;s open models, the safety logic does not hold; the real decider is the compute stock and the ability to deliver.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">US-China AI race</span><span class=\"tag\">Open weights</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Feishu and DingTalk Exit; the Personal Agent Takes Over AI Office Work Feishu is being folded into Doubao and DingTalk is ceding ground to Qwen Office (千问办公); enterprise IM has finished its historical mission. The center of AI office work shifts from organizational collaboration to individual productivity, with coding as the Agent&#x27;s foundation. 乱翻书 乱翻书 270.大厂押注AI办公，飞书和钉钉却先成了配角 AI Office Work Feishu DingTalk ByteDance Tencent Agents Agent ARR (Annual Recurring Revenue) CAPEX (Capital Expenditure) GEO (Generative Engine Optimization)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-01-luanfanshu-飞书钉钉退场-ai办公的主角成了个人agent/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/490e0c935e82c209-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"乱\"><span class=\"dur\">1:37:09</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">乱翻书</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0801</span></div>\n<h2>Feishu and DingTalk Exit; the Personal Agent Takes Over AI Office Work</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Feishu is being folded into Doubao and DingTalk is ceding ground to Qwen Office (<span lang=\"zh\">千问办公</span>); enterprise IM has finished its historical mission. The center of AI office work shifts from organizational collaboration to individual productivity, with coding as the Agent&#x27;s foundation.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI Office Work</span><span class=\"tag\">Feishu</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Dissected and Displayed After Hanging: What She Threatened Was Order, Not Money Mary Bateman, an 18th-century fraudster, worked through fortune-telling and a poisoned pie — not for the money, but for the pleasure of control. Her cons landed precisely on what people wanted to believe, and after her death her body was still put on display, exposing how much a society feared anyone who upended its order. Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford Cautionary Tales Arsenic and Lace: The Trial of the Yorkshire Witch - with Maddy Pelling Scams Psychological manipulation 18th century Historical crime Fraud defence Desire for power pleading the belly bottle conjurer ill wishes / well wishes\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-07-31-cautionary-约克郡女巫罪案-骗局的核心是权力欲/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b996da73a08744ac-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">43:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0731</span></div>\n<h2>Dissected and Displayed After Hanging: What She Threatened Was Order, Not Money</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Mary Bateman, an 18th-century fraudster, worked through fortune-telling and a poisoned pie — not for the money, but for the pleasure of control. Her cons landed precisely on what people wanted to believe, and after her death her body was still put on display, exposing how much a society feared anyone who upended its order.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Scams</span><span class=\"tag\">Psychological manipulation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"The Next Breakthrough in AI Research: Build a Physics of AI First, Then Let It Discover Architectures Itself Liu Ziming argues that real AFAI is not writing papers or rewriting training pipelines, but using the methods of physics to structure research first, then training a meta model to predict training curves, so that AI eventually discovers the next generation of architectures on its own. 张小珲·商业访谈录 张小珲·商业访谈录 149. 亲历中美neo labs资本狂潮，和清华刘子鸣聊：AI for AI、机制可解释性和Max Tegmark AI for AI Mechanistic Interpretability Meta Model Neo Labs Startup Fundraising Physics Thinking Physics of AI Mechanistic Interpretability Meta Model Recursive Superintelligence (RSI)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-07-30-zhangxiaojun-ai-for-ai-关键在于建立ai的物理学/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ba3f758130e67a9c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"张\"><span class=\"dur\">1:40:35</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">张小珲·商业访谈录</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0730</span></div>\n<h2>The Next Breakthrough in AI Research: Build a Physics of AI First, Then Let It Discover Architectures Itself</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Liu Ziming argues that real AFAI is not writing papers or rewriting training pipelines, but using the methods of physics to structure research first, then training a meta model to predict training curves, so that AI eventually discovers the next generation of architectures on its own.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>9</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI for AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Mechanistic Interpretability</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Brain Signals Show Up Seven Seconds Early, and the Researcher Still Says Free Will Survives The Libet and Haynes experiments show brain signals appearing half a second to seven seconds before ‘you decide’, but the researcher behind them denies this amounts to the absence of free will; compatibilism holds that will may be a macro-level emergent property of microscopic brain processes, the way temperature emerges from atoms. Science Vs Science Vs Free Will: Does It Exist? Free will Neuroscience Emergence Compatibilism Libet experiment Consciousness Emergence Clockwork universe fMRI (functional magnetic resonance imaging) EEG (electroencephalography)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-07-30-sciencevs-大脑早七秒做决定-自由意志依然存在/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a3282246c3202bd6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">31:49</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0730</span></div>\n<h2>Brain Signals Show Up Seven Seconds Early, and the Researcher Still Says Free Will Survives</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The Libet and Haynes experiments show brain signals appearing half a second to seven seconds before ‘you decide’, but the researcher behind them denies this amounts to the absence of free will; compatibilism holds that will may be a macro-level emergent property of microscopic brain processes, the way temperature emerges from atoms.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Free will</span><span class=\"tag\">Neuroscience</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"For AI Application Companies, Surviving Beats Innovating: Liblib Turns Cash-Flow Positive An AI application company dismissed as ‘只会抄袭+投流’ — nothing but copying plus buying traffic — has been cash-flow positive since May, with paid advertising accounting for only 4% of revenue. Founder Chen Mian (陈冕): the era of applications has not arrived yet, and the most important goal is to stay alive; between being the first to find PMF and finally winning the market lies an enormous distance. 晚点聊 LateTalk 晚点聊 175: 对话Liblib陈冕：关于活下来，以及所有接近死亡的时刻 AI applications startup survival cash flow paid acquisition originality PMF / product-market fit Reward model LTV / lifetime value GTM / go-to-market AI native ARR / annual recurring revenue\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-07-30-latetalk-ai-应用公司的最优解是活下来-而不是创新/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ff075962598c10e7-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"晚\"><span class=\"dur\">2:08:33</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">晚点聊 LateTalk</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0730</span></div>\n<h2>For AI Application Companies, Surviving Beats Innovating: Liblib Turns Cash-Flow Positive</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">An AI application company dismissed as ‘<span lang=\"zh\">只会抄袭</span>+<span lang=\"zh\">投流</span>’ — nothing but copying plus buying traffic — has been cash-flow positive since May, with paid advertising accounting for only 4% of revenue. Founder Chen Mian (<span lang=\"zh\">陈冕</span>): the era of applications has not arrived yet, and the most important goal is to stay alive; between being the first to find PMF and finally winning the market lies an enormous distance.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI applications</span><span class=\"tag\">startup survival</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"Tens of Thousands of Earthworks Under the Amazon, and a Civilization of Perhaps Three Million Using LiDAR to see through the rainforest canopy, archaeologists have found tens of thousands of earthworks in the southwestern Amazon, spread across more than 180,000 square kilometres, with a peak population that may have reached 3 million. Nature Podcast Nature Podcast Earthworks spotted in the Amazon hint at huge ancient civilization Amazon archaeology LiDAR Cephalopod fossils Fungal textiles Ancient civilizations Science findings LiDAR geoglyph siphuncle\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-07-29-naturepod-亚马逊地下古文明规模是预估的十倍/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/9b247947db171866-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">20:30</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Nature Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0729</span></div>\n<h2>Tens of Thousands of Earthworks Under the Amazon, and a Civilization of Perhaps Three Million</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Using LiDAR to see through the rainforest canopy, archaeologists have found tens of thousands of earthworks in the southwestern Amazon, spread across more than 180,000 square kilometres, with a peak population that may have reached 3 million.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Amazon archaeology</span><span class=\"tag\">LiDAR</span>\n</div></div></a>"]